hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're talking about  endospores endo spores are dormant tough  non reproductive parts of a white stage  that some bacteria can enter and you  might say well why is that desirable why  would that be favorable to bacteria to  enter a dormant non reproductive state  well in those spores are produced by  certain bacteria in response to very  harsh conditions in fact these  endospores can be made only by some gram  positive bacteria for example the  bacteria that cause tetanus that cause  anthrax that cause botulism these are  all types of bacteria that can produce  endospore structures when their  environmental conditions aren't very  favorable  now the endospores can protect the  bacteria against a wide variety of harsh  conditions for example they can protect  the bacteria against lack of nutrients  these bacteria may enter an endospore  part of their lifecycle if they are  experiencing starvation of carbon or  nitrogen so if they don't have carbon  and nitrogen that they need in order to  continue their life cycle instead they  may form endospores the endo spores are  also highly resistant to desiccation  this means extreme dryness it's stream'  lack of water the endospores are also  very resistant to extreme temperatures  and this is true for both hot and cold  temperatures also the endospores are  resistant to UV irradiation as well as  many chemicals now it is possible  certainly for the bacteria to leave the  endospore condition and return to what's  called the vegetative state this is  important for human health because for  example the bacteria that cause tetanus  you always hear you get tetanus if you  step on a rusty nail well that's because  the rusty nail may have endospores of  the bacteria that cause tetanus and once  the rusty nail introduces those  endospores deep into your skin tissues  which is a very favorable environment  those bacteria they can then germinate  or return to the vegetative state and  cause disease if you're interested in  learning more about endospores  please see my videos on score ulation  which is the process through which the  bacteria turn into endospores  you can also see my video on how to  stain for endospores so how to see them  under a light microscope and also my  video on bacterial growth and binary  fission to learn just how the bacteria  grow and divide once they have returned  to this vegetative state so that is it  for today and thank you for watching  biology professor
 

